Clock and Calendar - Concept & Step-by-Step Approach
Many competitive aptitude tests include questions based on clocks and calendars. Two common examples are:

Clock Problem: Given a time (in hours and minutes), determine the angle between the hour and minute hands.
Calendar Problem: Given a specific date, determine the day of the week or whether the year is a leap year.
Key constraints and points include:

For clock problems:
• The clock is circular (360°).
• The hour hand moves continuously, and its position depends on both the hour and the minutes.
• The minute hand moves 360° in 60 minutes (i.e., 6° per minute).

For calendar problems:
• Calendar calculations must account for leap years.
• Months have varying numbers of days.
• Some solutions use formulas (e.g., Zeller’s Congruence) or established lookup methods.

Section 2 — Intuition
For clock problems, the key insight is to translate the time into degrees:

The minute hand’s movement is straightforward (6° per minute).
The hour hand advances not only by the hour (30° per hour) but also fractionally by each minute (0.5° per minute).
The optimal approach uses these rate conversions to compute the absolute difference between the two angles, then selects the smaller angle (if needed).

For calendar problems, understanding the periodicity and adjustments for leap years is crucial. Standard algorithms (like Zeller’s Congruence) mathematically transform a date into a corresponding day index. Grasping the offsets and month/year adjustments is the “aha” moment for these problems.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
Description
A naive method for clock problems involves simulating the movement of the hands minute by minute until reaching the target time and then computing the corresponding angles. For the calendar problem, one might simply iterate through dates from a known reference date (e.g., 1 Jan 1900) until reaching the given date and count days to find the day of the week.

Pseudocode Example (Clock Problem)
Copy
plaintext
// Brute Force for Clock Angle Problem
// Simulate the clock until reaching the given time
function clockAngleBruteForce(hour, minute):
    // Set initial positions at 12:00
    currentHour = 12
    currentMinute = 0
    while (currentHour != hour or currentMinute != minute):
        currentMinute = currentMinute + 1
        if (currentMinute == 60):
            currentMinute = 0
            currentHour = currentHour + 1
            if (currentHour == 13):
                currentHour = 1
    // Calculate angles in the brute force manner
    minuteAngle = currentMinute * 6
    hourAngle = (currentHour % 12) * 30 + currentMinute * 0.5
    angleDifference = abs(hourAngle - minuteAngle)
    return min(angleDifference, 360 - angleDifference)
Time Complexity: O(total minutes simulated) which is O(1) for fixed maximum time (maximum 720 iterations in worst-case scenario) but conceptually not elegant.
Space Complexity: O(1) – only a few variables are used.
