Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
Optimal Clock Angle Calculation
Step-by-Step Algorithm:
Convert the given hour into the corresponding position on the 12-hour scale.
Calculate the minute hand’s angle:
• minuteAngle = minute * 6.
Calculate the hour hand’s angle:
• hourAngle = (hour % 12) * 30 + minute * 0.5.
Determine the absolute difference between these two angles.
If the difference is more than 180°, subtract it from 360° to get the smaller angle.
Return the final angle.
Pseudocode Example (Optimal - Clock)
Copy
plaintext
function clockAngleOptimal(hour, minute):
    // Normalize hour to 12-hour format
    normalizedHour = hour % 12
    // Compute angles
    minuteAngle = minute * 6               // 6° per minute
    hourAngle = normalizedHour * 30 + minute * 0.5  // 30° per hour + 0.5° per minute
    // Compute the absolute angle difference
    angleDifference = abs(hourAngle - minuteAngle)
    // Smaller angle between the two hands
    if (angleDifference > 180):
        angleDifference = 360 - angleDifference
    return angleDifference
Time Complexity: O(1) – Direct calculation with a fixed number of operations.
Space Complexity: O(1)
Optimal Calendar Day Calculation (Using Zeller’s Congruence)
Step-by-Step Algorithm:
If the month is January or February, adjust the month and year as follows:
• month = month + 12
• year = year - 1
Let: • q = day of the month
• m = adjusted month
• k = year % 100
• j = year / 100
Compute the day of the week using:
h = (q + floor(13*(m+1)/5) + k + floor(k/4) + floor(j/4) + 5*j) mod 7
h corresponds to:
• 0 = Saturday, 1 = Sunday, 2 = Monday, …, 6 = Friday.
Return the corresponding day.
Pseudocode Example (Optimal - Calendar)
Copy
